 and appropriate for  countries to consider  recognizing the taliban as the  government i think that  in some ways it's simple  if they show that they're broad-based  meaning they're accepting of other  political and social elements in society  to be part of a new order  if they respect men and women's rights  in afghanistan and allow women and girls  have to have access to education and  employment  if they allow a certain level of  freedoms such as media freedom which  actually still exists they haven't  cracked down totally on that  people still get on tv and criticize  them  which is amazing in some ways but  if they continue that  and if they crack down on international  organizations or terrorist groups that  are using afghanistan or want to use  afghanistan whether they're pro  tolerable or anti-talib and crack down  on those elements  whether they're anti-western or whether  they're anti-china or iran or russia  then i think that they will there's very  little room for the rest of the world  not to recognize them but those are some  really big conditions that they have to  meet well yes those are a lot of  conditions those are a lot of ifs  do you see any signs of those kinds of  advancements actually taking place  yes and no so you see  some girls going to school others not  you see boys going to school but then  women teachers not going to work or  women in general selectively go to work  but in general they don't and i think  the more presence  you have in afghanistan if the  international community is engaged if  the international media is still engaged  if there are some u.n agencies  functioning we would have a window into  afghanistan and we would know what the  taliban are doing and whether they're  changing or not if you shut down and you  close the country and you isolate the  country and you cut its lifelines then  obviously uh not only are we  jeopardizing their livelihoods and the  lives of afghans but we are also in some  ways  closing the door